From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 15:57:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: Read only the records a cursor step needs

Each step of a cursor, openCursor, continue, advance and
continuePrimaryKey, asked the backend for every record in the cursor's
range, with its value, and then searched the list for the first record
after the cursor's position. Iterating n records read about n * n / 2
rows: interleaved-cursors-small reads 4.65 GiB of values to visit
9.4 MiB, and on a DAYU200 three cursor tests timed out with the RDB
backend.

The Iterate operation now takes a count and a direction. The backend
returns the first count records of the range, or the last count ones in
reverse, still in ascending order, so the steps to iterate a cursor are
unchanged. For an object store source, whose keys are unique, the
cursor narrows its range to the records after its position, and to key
for continue(key), and asks for the step count. An index source still
reads its whole range, as the backends store no index records yet.

The SQLite backend now orders getAll and the cursor reads by key
instead of relying on the primary key index, and both backends apply
the limit.

Validation: host `cargo nextest run -p servo-storage-traits -p servo-storage` with SQLite: 56 passed, including the new test_restrict_bounds and test_indexeddb_iterate_count_and_reverse. On a DAYU200, a QuickJS checked-release build with both backends ran the whole IndexedDB/ directory with 2 processes and default timeouts, before and after this change, with each backend: with RDB, TIMEOUT 16 -> 6 of 820 and passing subtests 3,217 -> 3,225; with SQLite, TIMEOUT 15 -> 10 and 3,218 -> 3,224. The nine RDB cursor timeouts pass now (idbcursor_iterating 2.5 s, interleaved-cursors-small 19.5 s, interleaved-cursors-large 50 s, in window, worker and sharedworker); the tests that still time out did so in all four runs, and the remaining differences flip between runs of the same build. ./mach test-tidy passes. Host clippy: the workspace command fails building tikv-jemalloc-sys in this container, as it does without this change; with -p for the changed packages, all findings are in code this change doesn't touch.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; the agent found the cause, wrote the change and the tests, and measured it on the board)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
